--TEST--
`Core\Test::assertNeverCalled` holds for a method the exercise never reached, and its failure names the call it did find — `rule:testing/interaction-after-the-fact`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0079 § 11's other half. What this member is for is the assertion an
// expectation declared in advance would have reported from the wrong line: the
// call that should not have happened is named here, where the test says so.
interface Mailer {
    public function send(string $to): bool;
    public function purge(): void;
}

Mailer $mailer = Core\Test::double<Mailer>({
    send: fn(string $to): bool => true,
    purge: fn(): void => {},
});

echo $mailer->send("ada@example.test") ? "sent" : "refused", "\n";

// 1. The method the exercise never reached. A double records what it answers,
// so a method with no key in the record is a method nothing called.
Core\Test::assertNeverCalled($mailer, Mailer::purge);
echo "purge was never called\n";

// 2. One recorded call is enough to fail, and the failure names that call
// rather than only its count — the arguments are what a reader needs to find
// the line that made it.
try {
    Core\Test::assertNeverCalled($mailer, Mailer::send);
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $found) {
    echo $found->message, "\n";
}

// 3. The message option is prefixed exactly as it is on every other
// assertion, which is `rule:testing/assertions-are-typed`'s shape rather than
// anything this member decides for itself.
try {
    Core\Test::assertNeverCalled($mailer, Mailer::send, {message: "the mailer is disabled"});
} catch (Core\Test\Failure $labelled) {
    echo $labelled->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
sent
purge was never called
Core\Test::assertNeverCalled failed: `send` was called 1 time(s), the first as send("ada@example.test")
the mailer is disabled: Core\Test::assertNeverCalled failed: `send` was called 1 time(s), the first as send("ada@example.test")
